00:02:16.497 --> 00:02:17.330
SARA ROBINSON: Sure.

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:20.045
So the demo was of a
new API that we launched

00:02:20.045 --> 00:02:22.420
at the keynote this morning
called the Video Intelligence

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:25.960
API, which lets you basically
get granular data on what's

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:26.660
in your video.

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:28.284
So you send in a
video and it gives you

00:02:28.284 --> 00:02:30.160
back high level
data, some labels

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:31.320
on what's this video about.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:33.820
And then it also returns labels
for each scene in the video.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:35.920
It will tell you different
entities that it finds.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:38.170
So it could say, oh,
there's a dog in the scene

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:40.280
from second one to three.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:42.980
And it gives you that data
throughout your entire video.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:45.230
MARK MANDEL: So is this kind
of similar to Vision API,

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:46.312
but for basically video?

00:02:46.312 --> 00:02:47.270
SARA ROBINSON: Exactly.

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:49.478
Yeah, so if you're familiar
with the Vision API which

00:02:49.478 --> 00:02:50.920
gives the metadata
on images, this

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:52.570
does that for video,
which makes it

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:54.730
easy to search a large
library of video content

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:57.620
pretty easily to find the exact
scenes you're looking for.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:00.880
MARK MANDEL: So what was the
actual demo you did on stage?

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.500
SARA ROBINSON: So
that demo was an app

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.840
that I built with
another developer.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:08.490
And it's actually a Node.js app
that runs on Google App Engine.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:10.780
And it uses cloud
functions under the hood,

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:13.499
lots of Google Cloud
technology coming into play.

00:03:13.499 --> 00:03:15.790
So the way it works is there
was a Google Cloud Storage

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:17.830
bucket with all the videos.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:19.330
When you add a video
to that bucket,

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:21.160
there's a cloud
function listening

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:22.900
that sends a video
to the Video API

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:25.330
and then writes the
JSON response back

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:27.280
to a separate Cloud
Storage bucket.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:30.850
So the app is not calling
the Video API directly.

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:34.420
It just calls the front end that
retrieves the video metadata

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:36.190
from the Cloud Storage
bucket and then

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:39.794
the associated annotations
and displays them.

00:03:39.794 --> 00:03:41.710
And it makes it super
easy to implement search

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:44.590
because you have all that
annotation data in your JSON

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:45.670
response.

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:47.170
So you can search
your video library

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:49.260
and find the exact clips
you're looking for.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:50.977
It was definitely a
fun demo to built.

